# Terraform provider binaries and plugin cache. Large (hundreds of MB) and
# regenerated by `terraform init`. Recursive `**/` rather than pinned to one
# directory: a pinned pattern silently stops matching when the layout grows, and a
# provider binary then gets staged for commit.
**/.terraform/

# NO state file is committed, including seed's.
#
# Seed keeps local state for exactly one apply, because the bucket it lives in is
# what that apply creates. It is migrated into S3 immediately afterwards and the
# local copy deleted. Committing it would put resolved resource attributes into git
# history permanently, and "keep secrets out of seed" would become a rule someone
# has to remember forever rather than a property of the setup.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*

# Plan artifacts. Regenerable, and they contain the full resource diff.
*.tfplan
tf.plan

# Variable files can carry account-specific values. backend.hcl IS committed —
# it holds a bucket name and an account id, neither of which is secret, and the
# pairing between an account and its state location should be reviewable.
#
# `.tfvars.json` is listed separately on purpose: `*.tfvars` does NOT match
# `accounts.tfvars.json`, so without this line the account emails generated in CI
# would be committable.
*.tfvars
*.auto.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
!*.tfvars.example

# Rendered policies and Access Analyzer output. Regenerable from a plan.
policy-out/
plan.json
plan.txt

# Terraform crash logs can contain request/response bodies.
crash.log
crash.*.log

# macOS
.DS_Store

# Local environment — never commit credentials
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Scratch space for local-only helpers and rendered policy JSON used during
# validation. Nothing in here is part of the pipeline, and some of it reads
# credentials from a file outside the repo.
.local/
